So I did get the water. I went back. And... the shooting soon kind of died down and we went upstairs. We went upstairs. And then somebody was knocking on our door.

My mother opens the door and there stood a Russian soldier. And I think he was an officer. And my mother gave him a hug, kissed him. And she grew up in Slovakia, and she could speak Slovak. And she knew some Russian because during the First World War, a Russian soldier worked for the family. And so she learned Russian. And she welcomed him. And he came in and gave me chocolate, and we were liberated. We were liberated. And that was... the beginning of a new adventure. And my mother decided to work for the Russians. As an interpreter and an assistant. And... Well, this is another story, you know? Just what was happening, you know, because we had no food. No food. And I decided to look for food. I was wondering around. I was always wondering, all my life. [LAUGHING] And I went and I met a Russian soldier. And I saw in somebody's backyard a rabbit. I said to the Russian... [IMITATING GUNSHOTS] [LAUGHING] He took his gun out, shot the rabbit, went to get it and gave it to me. And my mother made a wonderful rabbit stew for dinner. That was the first and the last time I ate rabbit.

So that was the beginning of a new adventure.

INT: Were there any issues with the Russians coming in, were there any acts of retaliation that you witnessed? Were there any assaults against...

PH: They tried to rape my sister.

I was standing there with my sister and an old Russian soldier grabbed her and there was a cellar there, took her down, but my sister took off her watch and gave it to the Russian. Watch was a very valuable thing for Russians.

And... very quickly she came up so I knew there was no problem. She was-- Everything was okay. And then it was decided. My sister went down the road. And she went to a nunnery. There was a Catholic place there. And she asked protection. And they put her up there in a place... with other girls. And on the door it was written "Typhus. Do not enter." in Russian, in Hungarian, and in German. She stayed there for two weeks. Yeah. And so she was protected. And then, the end of the story was... But at one point, the siege of the castle was-- The Russians told us that, I don't know whether it's true or not, but they told us that they sent a radio message on a German radio to the defenders of the castle that we are here in the-- in a small village just outside. Break out and join us.

And apparently, the Germans-- the Hungarians and the Germans broke out.

But they were all massacred by the Russians, you know? And so when this was all over, mother and I started to go home.

And we walked down on the main street, we saw people... cutting up a horse because they wanted food. And then we started to walk on the main street. Suddenly a boy, a young man, joined us.

She says, "Can I help you with the luggage?" And my mother said, "Well, we can carry it, but..." "Please, I am running from the castle."

Oh, I said, "Yeah." So he admitted that he was one of the defenders. He was a member of the Volksbund, the German-- the Hungarian Germans. And I was a bit bloodthirsty. I said to mother, "Call the Russian, call the Russian, shoot the bastard, call the Russian." My mother said, "Peter, shut up." [LAUGHING] She says, "Keep quiet. Maybe he has a gun." And she was-- So nothing happened. So we walked, walked, walked and at the northern part of city. we walked through the ice. Half of the Danube was frozen. And on the other side, there was water, but the Russians had a boat and they took us. We walked home. My father was there and Ica was there. And soon my sister arrived. So end of my Holocaust story.

INT: Well, not quite. Tell me about the reunion when your family finally got together. What was that like for all of you?

PH: I have no idea.

