If two centuries did not convert people away from anti-Semitism, why would anything change now? We had to trust the communists who would not allow pogroms or anything like it. And all the fairy tales we heard in the school, this internationalism, everybody is going to be happy. Everybody is going to be looked after. It's going to be a paradise that is going to be built by the communists. And that lasted until 1953. And things began to change for me.

INT: Well speak about those interim years before things began to change for you. What was it like living under the Soviet regime?

PH: Well, I was a kid. I was a teenager. My main concern was school, chasing the girls. And despite of all the difficulties, there were serious problems. They would not accept me to university in '53 because my father was a merchant. And when the Communist Party and the Social Democratic Party forcefully united, my dad foolishly mentioned when they asked him, "What were you doing in 1945?" He said, "Well, I was afraid to go out because people were here and they're rounded up. [RUSSIAN] Which means "a little work," but in Russian language. [RUSSIAN] They rounded up people and they took them to Russia for a few years. And so because he said something terrible about the liberating army, the Red Army, he was instantly kicked out from the party. because I was... a third class citizen, you know? First class-- In a communist country, first class, the son of a industrial worker. Second best, the child of a farmer or a peasant. Third, somebody worked for the government, maybe, or whatever. And if you were the son of an industrialist or an aristocrat or... [UNCLEAR] Not allowed to the university.

So it was difficult. But things began to change very quickly in '53. And by 1956, I was carrying a gun. I participated in a revolution, in my eyes, for a better, a freer country. It was not for the expulsion of my old friends. To get rid of Stalinism. We were dreaming. Some of us kids were dreaming of a socialist, and honor, and a healthy socialist country, not a Stalinist. And I fought for them until it was over, and then I came around here.

INT: Thank you. Before I have you leaving, I want to know what was going on from 1953 to 1956 that made you pick up a gun? Can you elaborate, please?

PH: Well, in 1953, Imre Nagy became the Prime Minister and the Stalinist leader of of Hungary was pushed into the background and there was more freedom. The newspapers were telling more honestly what is happening in the countryside. And the literary paper was published, and every week we were running to get the new paper. We felt much freer and freer all the time. And... ...freedom was coming, and we were not afraid of the police anymore that they are going to do something. And eventually, the Stalinist leaders were removed. And... I was working as a law student in a factory, and there were youth movement where people were telling the real problems of the country, etc. And I went to a Communist Party meeting where somebody reported on-- about this meeting and I stood up. I said, "What you are saying is a lie. Nobody was saying these things. These were honorable people who want to make Hungary a better place, a better place to live." And so...

But, you see, Buda, there were this revolution that came about was mainly in Budapest. And some of the cities, the countryside remained very quiet. There were no shooting or anything like that. And so there were two streams what was going on? Those who wanted to have a just socialist country, not a Stalinist country. And there were others wanting to have the old system back. They wanted to have a country which was Hungarian. So more nationalistic.

